Samba File & Printer Sharing
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install samba - Install Samba package
    
    sudo dnf install cups - Install CUPS for printer sharing (optional)

2. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=samba - Allow Samba traffic
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=139/tcp - Allow SMB port
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=445/tcp - Allow SMB port
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Apply firewall changes

3. User & Directory Setup

    sudo mkdir /path/to/share - Create shared directory
    
    sudo smbpasswd -a username - Add Samba user (use existing Linux user)

4. Configuration File

    sudo nano /etc/samba/smb.conf - Edit main config file
    
    Example Share Configuration:
    
    text
    [share_name]
    path = /path/to/shared/folder
    browseable = yes
    read only = no
    valid users = username
5. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start smb - Start Samba service
    
    sudo systemctl enable smb - Enable on boot
    
    sudo systemctl restart smb - Restart after config changes

6. Printer Sharing

    sudo systemctl start cups - Start CUPS printing service
    
    sudo systemctl enable cups - Enable CUPS on boot
    
    Add to smb.conf:
    
    text
    [printers]
    path = /var/spool/samba
    printable = yes
    guest ok = no
7. Testing & Connection

    testparm - Validate configuration syntax
    
    smbclient //server_name/share_name -U username - Connect from Linux client
    
    Windows: \\server_name\share_name - Access from Windows Explorer

NFS Server Setup
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install nfs-utils - Install NFS server package

2. Directory Setup

    sudo mkdir /shared - Create shared directory
    
    sudo chmod -R 755 /shared - Set appropriate permissions

3. Export Configuration

    sudo nano /etc/exports - Edit exports file
    
    Example Entry:
    
    /shared *(rw,sync,no_root_squash) - Share to all hosts
    
    /shared 192.168.1.0/24(rw,sync) - Share to specific network

4. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start nfs-server - Start NFS service
    
    sudo systemctl enable nfs-server - Enable on boot
    
    sudo exportfs -arv - Export all configured shares

5. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=nfs - Allow NFS traffic
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=mountd - Allow mount service
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=rpc-bind - Allow RPC binding
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Apply firewall changes

6. Client Access

    sudo mount -t nfs server_name:/shared /mnt/mount_point - Mount on client
    
    Auto-mount: Add to /etc/fstab on client:
    
    server_name:/shared /mnt/mount_point nfs defaults 0 0

FTP Server Configuration (vsftpd)
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install vsftpd - Install Very Secure FTP Daemon

2. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start vsftpd - Start FTP service
    
    sudo systemctl enable vsftpd - Enable on boot
    
    sudo systemctl restart vsftpd - Restart after config changes

3. Configuration File

    sudo nano /etc/vsftpd/vsftpd.conf - Edit main config

    Key Options:
    
    anonymous_enable=YES/NO - Allow anonymous access
    
    local_enable=YES - Allow local users
    
    local_root=/path/to/ftp - Set FTP root directory
    
    listen_port=2121 - Change default port (optional)

4. Firewall Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ftp - Allow FTP traffic
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Apply firewall changes

5. User Management

    sudo useradd -m ftpuser - Create FTP user
    
    sudo passwd ftpuser - Set user password

6. Testing & Troubleshooting

    FTP Client: Connect using FileZilla or command line
    
    Log Checking: journalctl -xe | grep vsftpd - Check for errors
    
    Test Connection: ftp your_server_ip - Test from client
